\description{
Construct a sparse feature x cell count matrix from one or more genomic
fragment files. Rows of the returned matrix correspond to features
(genomic intervals supplied in \code{features}) and columns correspond to cells.
Each entry records the number of fragments from that cell overlapping that
feature.
}
\section{Counting}{
Two counting schemes are supported:
\itemize{
\item Paired Insertion Counting (PIC, the default, \code{pic = TRUE}): each fragment
contributes at most one count per feature, regardless of whether one or
both insertion sites (fragment start and end) fall inside the feature.
See Martens et al. (2024) \doi{10.1038/s41592-023-02103-7}.
\item Insertion counting (\code{pic = FALSE}): each Tn5 insertion site is counted
independently, so a fragment with both ends inside a feature contributes 2
counts.
}
}

\section{Backends}{
Quantification is performed by one of two backends:
\itemize{
\item \code{fragtk} (default, \code{fragtk = TRUE}): fast, memory-efficient Rust
implementation suitable for large feature sets or many fragment files.
Requires the \code{fragtk} executable on \code{PATH} (or an explicit path passed as
a character string to \code{fragtk}). See \url{https://crates.io/crates/fragtk}.
\item R implementation (\code{fragtk = FALSE}): a pure-R path built on
\code{\link[Rsamtools:TabixFile-class]{Rsamtools::TabixFile()}}. Lower overhead for small feature sets (for
example, a handful of peaks) and useful when \code{fragtk} is not installed.
}
}

\section{Dispatch}{
\code{FeatureMatrix} is a generic function able to be called using multiple
inputs. Methods are provided for
\link[SeuratObject:Seurat-class]{SeuratObject::Seurat}, \linkS4class{ChromatinAssay5}, \linkS4class{Fragment2}, and for
a character fragment file path (\code{default}). Higher-level methods extract
the fragment information they need and delegate to the \code{default} method,
which operates directly on a single fragment file path.

When multiple fragment files are associated with an assay (for example, a
merged dataset), each is quantified independently and the resulting
matrices are merged by adding counts for shared cells and features. Cells
that appear in only a subset of fragment files are still represented in the
output (zero-filled for the files in which they are absent).
}

\section{On-disk output via BPCells}{
Setting \code{bpcells = TRUE} writes the count matrix to disk in BPCells format
at \code{bpcells.dir} and returns a \code{BPCells::IterableMatrix} instead of an
in-memory sparse matrix. This is recommended for large datasets where the
dense count matrix would not fit in memory. With the \code{fragtk} backend, the
matrix is streamed from fragtk's 10x Matrix Market output into BPCells
without materializing a dense in-memory copy. With the R backend, the
matrix is built in memory first and then written out; the benefit there is
on-disk persistence, not reduced memory use. \code{bpcells.dir} is required when
\code{bpcells = TRUE} and must point to a directory that does not already exist
or is empty. Requires the \code{BPCells} package.
}

